Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the denominator is $x^2$, let's divide by $x^2$.

In the numerator, let's divide by $\sqrt{x^4}$, since for any value, $x^2=\sqrt{x^4}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\sqrt{4x^4-x}}{2x^2+3}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{\sqrt{4x^4-x}}{\sqrt{x^4}}}{\dfrac{2x^2+3}{x^2}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x^2=\sqrt{x^4}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\sqrt{\dfrac{4\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}-\dfrac{1\cancel x}{\cancel x\cdot x^3}}}{\dfrac{2\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}+\dfrac{3}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\sqrt{4-\dfrac{1}{x^3}}}{2+\dfrac{3}{x^2}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{\sqrt{4-0}}{2+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to-\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\sqrt{4}}{2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2}{2}
\\\\
&=1
\end{align}$
